{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:labor:audit","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:7a5a9796463909fda5280f1c767dd9930ee7b722598fcdec332f897bde2f6f4a","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1.1},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.9},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.8}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"RESOLVING SERIES. IMF WEO (or OECD Economic Outlook) real GDP growth aggregated across OECD-38 + China on PPP weights. Three construction decisions each move the answer by more than 3pp of probability mass.\n\n(1) WEIGHT BASIS. OECD-38 ~ $70T 2025 PPP, China ~ $40T, so China weight ~36%. IMF aggregates are CHAIN-weighted on current PPP, under which China's weight drifts to ~38-40% by 2036 and the composite growth rate rises; the Section lock says constant 2025 USD PPP, which FREEZES the weight and LOWERS the composite by ~5-10bp. I used fixed 2025 weights.\n(2) CHINA IS REPORTED, NOT TRUE. CQ3 resolves on NBS-reported real GDP as reprinted by IMF. Independent work (including Rhodium, cited in 2A) argues reported real growth exceeds a deflator-consistent estimate by ~1-1.5pp. At a 0.36 weight that is +0.35 to +0.55pp on the composite relative to any \"true growth\" forecast. Direction: UP. This is the single largest swing factor in CQ3 and it is pure measurement.\n(3) ICP PPP REVISIONS. The ICP 2026 round (results ~2029) will restate China's PPP level as ICP 2021 did; a ±5% restatement of China's PPP GDP is ±1.8pp of weight and ±2bp of composite — small, but it also makes the \"constant 2025 PPP\" anchor itself a revisable object.\n\nFERMI. OECD leg: 2A gives per-capita baseline 1.0% -> 0.6%; OECD population growth 0.2-0.3%/yr (migration-dependent) implies aggregate ~0.8-0.9% before AI. AI-Q6's central standalone regime (+0.15 to +0.59pp) and the demand-pull coupling (labour scarcity raises the adoption return) put OECD 2032-36 at ~1.2%. Two measurement offsets: AI capex is measured investment, and the 2032-36 window most likely sits AFTER the buildout peak, so the investment CONTRIBUTION turns negative in growth-rate terms (impairments are balance-sheet, not flow, so the 66% AI-Q4 marginal subtracts nothing directly) — call it -0.1 to -0.3pp; against that, fast-falling hedonic price indices for AI servers and capitalised software flatter real investment and hence measured real GDP, +0.05 to +0.15pp. China leg: reported ~4.5-5% now, 2A's -2.7pp/yr aging drag is a 2025-2050 average, so 2032-36 reported ~3.0%.\nComposite P50 = 0.64 x 1.2 + 0.36 x 3.0 = 0.77 + 1.08 = 1.85 -> 1.9.\nP10 (AI capex bust echo plus Chinese property/fiscal hard landing: OECD 0.3, China 1.5) = 0.73, but a five-YEAR average that low is deep tail; P10 = 1.1. P90 (breakout plus Chinese reflation: OECD 2.0, China 4.0) = 2.72 -> 2.8.\n\nIDENTITY THAT DOES NOT CLOSE (reported in coupling_tension and applied here): 2A's \"+0.4pp/yr healthy-aging offset ... YET aging still slows global growth by ~-1.1pp/yr\" reads as -1.1 being NET of the +0.4. Any effort that adds the +0.4 to a -1.1 baseline double-counts and inflates coupled growth by ~+0.4pp. I used the net reading, which is why my P50 is below a naive 2.3%.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:51.253Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"TÜİK publishes Türkiye calendar-2029 core CPI (ex food and energy, annual average) below 8.0%, and OECD.Stat's OECD-total core aggregate narrows to within 0.25pp of the G7 core aggregate.\",\"by_when\":\"Mar 31 2030 (Jan-2030 print plus OECD aggregate revision)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes ~0.35pp of the composition floor that carries my CQ2 OECD conjunct and my CQ6-C mass. CQ2 falls from 49 to ~41 (conjunct a from 0.86 to ~0.72); CQ6-C falls ~6pp to ~24 with the mass going to B (~53) and D (~10). CQ4 P50 unchanged.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat publishes SES 2026 median gross hourly earnings by NACE section showing section Q's median falling in real terms (national HICP-deflated) in a majority of reporting members while Q employment rises more than 8% over 2022-2026.\",\"by_when\":\"Jun 30 2029 (SES 2026 release)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Confirms the entrant-composition drag on CQ1's second conjunct directly rather than by inference. Conjunct (b) falls from 0.20 to ~0.12; CQ1 falls from 13 to ~9. No effect on CQ3/CQ4. If instead Q's median RISES in real terms by more than 6pp cumulative over 2022-2026, conjunct (b) goes to ~0.32 and CQ1 rises to ~20.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"BLS introduces 2030-census-based CPS population controls with the January 2032 employment situation release and publishes a documented unemployment-RATE revision for the overlap period.\",\"by_when\":\"Feb 28 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"If the rate revision is under 0.2pp (the historical pattern: level revisions, rate-neutral), CQ1's first conjunct holds at ~0.58-0.62 and CQ1 stays ~13. If the redesign raises the measured rate by 0.4pp or more (e.g. via response-rate repair or a reworked 65+ labour-force question), conjunct (a) falls to ~0.42 and CQ1 falls to ~9; it would also lift CQ6-C and CQ6-D a combined ~3pp at B's expense.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"ICE Data Indices announces any methodology change, reconstitution, rebranding, or discontinuation of the ICE BofA MOVE Index, or changes its tenor weights.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ5's first conjunct becomes partly unresolvable as specified and the auditor default is NO. CQ5 falls from 24 to ~15. No effect on CQ3/CQ6; small downward effect on CQ4's P90 only insofar as it removes an observable stress gauge.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The IEA Energy and AI / Electricity update reports OECD-38 plus China operational data-centre electricity at or above 750 TWh for calendar 2029 on a strict operational definition, WHILE PJM, ERCOT and EirGrid interconnection queue times remain above 4 years.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Activates CQ6-C's grid-wall leg, which my own capex arithmetic currently rejects at AI-Q3's P50. CQ6-C rises ~5pp to ~35, B falls to ~44; CQ3 P50 falls ~0.15pp to ~1.75 and P10 to ~1.0; CQ4 P90 rises ~0.1pp on the term-premium channel.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 1 audit seat, effort 'labor'."}}